  "image_name": "0a16bcaf5cf1bfaa9a5b74fc2a397771.png",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes two-way communication between adipose tissue, sensory nerves, and the brain. At the center is a white adipocyte cluster supplied by blood vessels and associated with beige adipocytes, macrophages, and a Treg cell. The main idea is that adipose tissue can signal outward through afferent sensory nerves, while nerve endings can also feed signals back to adipose tissue.\n\nOn the adipose side, the figure highlights a range of secreted factors reported to influence afferent nerves, including leptin, growth factors, fatty-acid–related lipids, prostaglandins, inflammatory cytokines, and immune mediators. These molecules may come directly from adipocytes or from adipose-resident cells such as immune cells. The sensory terminals within the tissue then relay information along an afferent pathway to the brain, shown by dashed blue projections.\n\nIn the opposite direction, the nerve terminals release CGRP and substance P locally into the adipose tissue. These neuropeptides are indicated as factors that can affect adipose cells and their local microenvironment. Overall, the figure depicts adipose tissue not just as a storage organ but as an active sensory and signaling hub that communicates with the nervous system through immune, metabolic, and neuropeptide-mediated signals.",
